    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nLet's take a number, say, [MATH: -3.] We can depict it on a number line as follows:\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/639fbaffa133fb983eef5d2453eeedba]\n\nContent:\nThe distance between the number [MATH: -3] and [MATH: 0] is known as the absolute value of [MATH: -3.] We write it as\n[MATH: |-3| = {\\color{red}3}.]\n\nContent:\nSimilarly, [MATH: |\\,2\\,|], the absolute value of the number [MATH: 2], is the distance between [MATH: 2] and [MATH: 0]:\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/197c5c28024cc300e7086bfff10e313a]\n\nContent:\nWe write [MATH: |\\,2\\,| = {\\color{red}2}.]\n\nContent:\nSo, the absolute value simply\n\nContent:\n- removes the minus sign, if the number is negative, or\n- does nothing, if the number is non-negative.\n\nContent:\nSometimes, we can be asked to evaluate an expression involving the absolute value function, like [MATH: |x +1| + 5] when [MATH: x=-3.] In this case, we evaluate the expression inside the absolute value before simplifying the rest of the expression:\n[MATH: |x + 1|+ 5|=;|(\u2212 3) + 1|+ 5|=;|\u2212 2|+ 5|=; 2 + 5|=; 7]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Evaluating an Expression With One Absolute Value\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: |x - 6| + 2 x +3] for [MATH: x = -3.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe substitute [MATH: x = -3] and then evaluate the absolute value before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n[MATH: |x \u2212 6|+ 2x + 3|=;|\u2212 3 \u2212 6|+ 2(\u2212 3) + 3|=;|\u2212 9|+ 2(\u2212 3) + 3|=; 9 + 2(\u2212 3) + 3|=; 9 \u2212 6 + 3|=; 6]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 6 \u2212|x \u2212 9|] for [MATH: x = \u2212 2.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 5]\nb. [MATH: 5]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 2]\nd. [MATH: 16]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 16]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: t = \u2212 1,] then [MATH: |t^{2} + t \u2212 1|+ t =][FREE_ENTRY 1].\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: p = 3,] then [MATH: |3 \u2212 5^{p \u2212 2}|+ 7 =][FREE_ENTRY 1].\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Example: Evaluating an Expression With Two Absolute Values\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: |2 - x| + 1 + 5|x|] for [MATH: x = 5.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe substitute [MATH: x = 5] and then evaluate the absolute values before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n[MATH: |2 \u2212 x|+ 1 + 5|x||=;|2 \u2212 5|+ 1 + 5|5||=;|\u2212 3|+ 1 + 5(5)|=; 3 + 1 + 25|=; 4 + 25|=; 29]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: t = 0,] then [MATH: |t \u2212 2|\u2212|t + 3|=][FREE_ENTRY 1].\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: p = 1,] then [MATH: |p|^{4} \u2212 4|p \u2212 3|^{2} + 6 =][FREE_ENTRY 1].\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: y = 0,] then [MATH: 2|1 \u2212 3^{y}|\u2212|2^{y} \u2212 4|=][FREE_ENTRY 1]."
